Enemy AI planning

Steps:
1. Gather context
2. Compute possible moves - compute values
3. Compute possible primary actions - compute values
4. Prune candidates to top X per category (prevent too many iterations)
5. Rank values against priorities / weights
6. Commit action.


Step 0: Gather context (cheap caches)

	Precompute per planning pass:
	Player unit list + positions
	Enemy unit list + positions
	For each player unit: its threat area (tiles it can attack from its current position, or move+attack if your player can do that)
	For each candidate move cell of the acting enemy: distance to nearest player, cover/terrain modifiers, etc.
	This lets you score risk/position quickly without recomputing everything for each candidate.

Step 1: Enumerate candidate move endpoints

Compute the enemy’s reachable cells (plus “stay put”).

Optional filters (huge perf win):
	Remove cells that are strictly dominated:
		same distance to all relevant players but worse terrain
	Limit to top N cells by a cheap heuristic:
		closest-to-best-target
		best defensive terrain
		nearest objective

Step 2: From each endpoint, enumerate candidate actions (These computed by threat range, not recomputed for each move tile)

For each move endpoint m:
	Attack candidates: for each enemy attack action, list valid targets from m
	Heal candidates: valid ally targets from m
	Disable/debuff candidates: valid targets from m
	Wait candidate: no action (or defend)
Represent each as a concrete candidate:
	Candidate = (moveEndpoint, actionType, target)
This is key: your scoring becomes consistent.

Step 3: Score each candidate with a structured scoring function

Split into two parts (helps avoid double-counting):

A) PositionScore(moveEndpoint)
	Things you can score without simulating an action:
		Safety / exposure: how many player units threaten this tile (or threaten after move if that’s part of player rules)
		Terrain benefit: cover/avoid/defense
		Formation / support: near allies (if you want)
		Goal pressure: proximity to objectives / choke points
B) ActionScore(action, target, fromCell)
	Immediate effect estimates:
	Damage / heal / status utility
	Kill bonus (very high)
	Disable bonus (scaled by target value)
	Counterattack / retaliation penalty
	Overkill waste penalty (small)
Then:
	TotalScore = PositionScore + ActionScore

Step 4: Pick best candidate (with tie-breakers)

Tie-breakers help AI look less random:
	prefer kills
	prefer less movement (more stable visuals)
	prefer higher safety if scores close
	small randomness within top few (optional)

Step 5: Build the plan from the winning candidate

Return:
	MoveTargetCell (maybe null)
	PrimaryAction + target (maybe null)
	Optional: “face direction”, “use ability variant”, etc.


Must-have

Kill bonus (huge)
	Expected net HP swing:
		damageDealt - damageTakenByCounter
	Death risk penalty:
		if tile is threatened by strong player units, penalize
	Target value weighting
		attacking a healer > attacking a tank (or whatever your roles are)

Nice-to-have (still cheap)
	Focus fire bonus
		prefer targets already damaged (increases kill likelihood)
	Isolation penalty
		don’t run far from allies unless you’re an assassin-type
	Objective pressure
		path toward capture points / player backline


Minimax / lookahead (with shallow depth)

What it is: simulate “enemy does X, player responds” for 1 ply (or 2 ply).
	Pros
		Much smarter positioning
		Captures traps / sacrifices
	Cons
		Expensive and needs a fast simulator
		Hard if combat has lots of randomness
		Use it if: later, when you have a stable fast combat resolver and want “hard mode”.

Candidate structure:
Candidate = (MoveEndPoint m, Action A, Target t)

PositionScore: what it should include in FE-style (move+act)
Since the unit will end the activation on m, PositionScore should estimate next player phase danger + strategic value.

Minimum viable PositionScore terms

	Exposure / risk
		count (or weight) how many player units can attack m next turn
		heavier penalty if “high damage” units threaten it
	Terrain / tile defense
		cover/avoid/def bonuses, choke points, etc.
	Ally support
		bonus for being within X of allies (prevents lone suicides)
	Objective proximity
		(optional now) closer to goal, threaten capture points, etc.
	Important: avoid double-counting

If ActionScore already includes “counterattack damage,” PositionScore should mostly be about future damage (next player turn), not “damage taken during this combat.”


ActionScore: what it should include (immediate outcome)
	Attack action
		Core terms:
			Kill bonus (big)
			Expected damage dealt (or deterministic damage)
			Expected counter damage taken (penalty)
			Target value weighting
				healer / high offense / low HP etc.
		Nice but optional:
			Focus fire bonus (prefer finishing damaged units)
				focus fire setup - small bonus favoring target with multiple attack spots open.
			Overkill waste penalty (small)

	Heal action
		Core:
			Effective heal amount (actual missing HP healed)
			Save-from-death bonus (if ally would likely die next player turn)
			Healed unit value (healing a carry > healing a tank, depending)
		Disable / debuff action
			Core:
				Disable strength (stun > slow, etc.)
				Target value
				“Prevents kill” estimate
					e.g., disabling a unit that threatens many enemies is valuable